\[ 1 \text{ hour} = 60 \text{ minutes} \times 60 \text{ seconds/minute} = 3600 \text{ seconds} \]

Next, we use the equation \( y = 1.5x \) to find the distance \( y \) when \( x = 3600 \) seconds:
\[ y = 1.5 \times 3600 \]

Calculating this:
\[ y = 5400 \text{ meters} \]

