Problem:
In the diagram, $K$, $O$ and $M$ are the centers of the three semi-circles. Also, $OC = 32$ and $CB = 36$.

[asy]
pair A, K, O, C, M, B, X, Y, Z;
O=(0,0);
C=(32,0);
M=(50,0);
B=(68,0);
A=(-68,0);
K=(A+C)/2;
X=(0,68);
Y=(-18,50);
Z=(50,18);
path nom, bigc, middlec, smallc;
nom=A--B--(100,100)--(-100,100)--cycle;
bigc=A..X..B--cycle;
middlec=A..Y..C--cycle;
smallc=C..Z..B--cycle;
fill(bigc, gray(.5));
fill(middlec, white);
fill(smallc, white);
draw(smallc);
draw(middlec);
draw(bigc);
draw(A--B);
label("A", A, S);
label("K", K, S);
label("O", O, S);
label("M", M, S);
label("C", C, S);
label("B", B, S);
dot(K);
dot(O);
dot(M);
[/asy]

What is the area of the semi-circle with center $K$?

Solution:
answer:  To find the area of the semi-circle with center \( K \), we first need to determine the radius of the semi-circle. The radius \( r \) is half of the diameter \( CB \).

Given:
\[ CB = 36 \]

The radius \( r \) is:
\[ r = \frac{CB}{2} = \frac{36}{2} = 18 \]

The area \( A \) of a full circle is given by the formula:
\[ A = \pi r^2 \]

Substituting the radius \( r = 18 \):
\[ A = \pi (18)^2 = 324\pi \]

Since we are dealing with a semi-circle, the area of the semi-circle is half of the area of the full circle. Therefore, the area of the semi-circle with center \( K \) is:
\[ \text{Area of semi-circle} = \frac{1}{2} \times 324\pi = 162\pi \]

Thus, the area of the semi semi with center \( K \) is:
\[
\boxed{162\pi} \\]
