Solution:
answer:  We know that $P(z)$ has a zero $z_0$ with $|z_0|=1$, so $P(z)$ can be written as $P(z)=4(z-z_0)Q(z)$ for some polynomial $Q(z)$. Since $|z_0|=1$, we have $P(1)=4(1-z_0)Q(1)$. Therefore, $P(1)$ is a multiple of $4(1-z_0)$.

Now, we need to find the sum of all values of $P(1)$ over all the polynomials with these properties. Since $P(1)$ is a multiple of $4(1-z_0)$, we can write $P(1)=k\cdot4(1-z_0)$ for some integer $k$. The sum of all values of $P(1)$ is the sum of all values of $k\cdot4(1-z_0)$ over all possible values of $k$.

