Problem:
A block of cheese in the shape of a rectangular solid measures $10$ cm by $13$ cm by $14$ cm. Ten slices are cut from the cheese. Each slice has a width of $1$ cm and is cut parallel to one face of the cheese. The individual slices are not necessarily parallel to each other. What is the maximum possible volume in cubic cm of the remaining block of cheese after ten slices have been cut off?

Solution:
answer:  To find the maximum possible volume of the remaining block of cheese after ten slices have been cut off, we need to consider the dimensions of the remaining block. Each slice reduces the width of the block by 1 cm, so after ten slices, the width of the remaining block will be $13 - 10 = 3$ cm.

The height of the remaining block will remain unchanged at 14 cm, and the length will remain 10 cm.

The volume of the remaining block is given by the product of its length, width, and height:

\[
\text{Volume} = \text{length} \times \text{width} \times \text{height} = 10 \times 3 \times 14
\]

Calculating this:

\[
10 \times 3 = 30
\]
\[
30 \times 14 = 420
\]

Therefore, the maximum possible volume of the remaining block of cheese after ten slices have been cut off is \(\boxed{420) cubic cubic..

Final Answer: The final answer is $420. I hope it is correct.
